# {{projectName}}

A website made with [Uniweb](https://uniweb.io): an open-source React framework and a connected
visual authoring platform.

**Developers define the vocabulary. Authors compose with it. Uniweb keeps both sides in sync.**

This project and the Uniweb app are two interfaces to the same website. Here, in code and git,
you build its **foundation**: React components, called section types, and the schemas of its
structured content. In the app, authors compose pages from those section types and manage records
through those schemas: visually, with live previews, and with no markdown, YAML or git to learn.
You build the components. Uniweb builds the authoring experience.
{{#if cloned}}

This project was cloned from a site in the Uniweb app (`uniweb clone`), so it is already in sync
with it. Its foundation is a registered one, not a folder of this project.
{{/if}}

## Quick start

{{#if packages.length}}
```bash
pnpm install
pnpm dev          # prints the address it serves the site at
```

Edit a page or a section type and the page updates as you save. With npm, use `npm install`,
`npm run dev` and `npx uniweb`.
{{else}}
This workspace started empty. Add a foundation and a site, then run them:

```bash
npx uniweb add project <name>    # a foundation in <name>/src, a site in <name>/site
pnpm install
pnpm uniweb dev                  # prints the address it serves the site at
```
{{/if}}

## What's here

| Folder | What it holds |
|---|---|
{{#each packages}}
| `{{path}}/` | {{#if foundation}}a foundation: section types in `sections/`, written in React, and any data schemas in `schemas/`{{/if}}{{#if extension}}an extension: more section types, loaded beside a foundation{{/if}}{{#if site}}a site: its pages in `pages/`, with `site.yml` and `theme.yml`{{/if}} |
{{/each}}
| `AGENTS.md` | the manual for this project and its version of Uniweb, for you and for AI agents |

Each markdown file in a site's `pages/` is one section of a page. Its frontmatter names a
section type, and the component receives the content already parsed. For example:

```markdown
---
type: Hero
---

# Build something great

Edit this text, not the component.
```

```jsx
// sections/Hero/index.jsx
import { H1, P } from '@uniweb/kit'

export default function Hero({ content }) {
  return (
    <div className="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-6">
      <H1 text={content.title} className="text-4xl font-bold text-heading" />
      <P text={content.paragraphs} className="mt-4 text-body" />
    </div>
  )
}
```

`text-heading` and `text-body` are theme tokens. A site's `theme.yml` sets their colors, so one
component works in light and dark sections, and on every site that uses it.

## Connect the authoring app

Start with a normal project, and connect the app when you want visual authoring:

```bash
pnpm uniweb login       # sign in to your Uniweb account
pnpm uniweb sync        # the first sync brings the site and its foundation into the app
```

From then on, the CLI is the bridge between the two workflows:

| Command | What it does |
|---|---|
| `pnpm uniweb push` | sends your section types, schemas and content to the app: a draft, not live |
| `pnpm uniweb pull` | brings authors' edits into your files, as a git merge |
| `pnpm uniweb sync` | takes your teammates' commits and the authors' edits, then pushes yours |
| `pnpm uniweb publish` | pushes, then takes the site live on Uniweb hosting, a paid service |

Authors never need to think about synchronization: in the app, their changes appear live. On
your side, edits to different sections combine, and nothing overwrites an author's edit. When
the same section changed on both sides, `sync` stops at a git conflict for you to settle. Like
git, a push sends what you have committed; `--commit` commits the site's content first.

### Structured content, editable without an admin app

A section type declares the data it shows, by schema, in its `meta.js`:

```js
export default {
  data: {
    people: '@std/person',   // a standard schema, from @uniweb/schemas
    events: '@/event'        // this foundation's own, in schemas/event.yml
  }
}
```

A standard schema needs `@uniweb/schemas` as a dependency of the foundation. In the app, authors
get forms and records for those schemas. No separate admin application is needed to make the
data editable.

## Other ways to use it

The framework works on its own, too.

- **Content in files only.** If you manage the content yourself, the site needs no Uniweb account.
  `pnpm build` makes each site's `dist/`, a static site for any host, and `pnpm uniweb deploy`
  ships it to GitHub Pages, Cloudflare Pages, Netlify, Vercel or S3 + CloudFront.
- **Foundations for your clients' sites.** If the sites belong to your clients, who make and edit
  them in the app, this project is where you build and test the foundation.
  `pnpm uniweb register` publishes it to Uniweb for their sites.

## Working with AI agents

A Uniweb project gives an agent a system to work within, its section types, schemas and content
conventions, instead of a blank canvas. What an agent builds here, authors can use in the app
after the next sync.

[AGENTS.md](./AGENTS.md) is written for developers and AI agents alike. Many coding agents read it
on their own when they start in this folder; point any other agent at it first.
`npx uniweb@latest update` moves the project to the latest release of Uniweb, and AGENTS.md with
it.

Some prompts to try:

**Converting an existing design:**
```
I have a landing page design in `homepage.jsx`. Convert it into this Uniweb
project. Decompose each visual section into a section type in the foundation,
and extract all text content into markdown files in the site's pages/home/.
The final page should render identically to the original. See AGENTS.md.
```

**Adding a section type:**
```
Create a Pricing section type with 3-column cards. It should work in light and
dark sections. Use content.items for the pricing tiers — each item has a title,
a paragraph (the price), and a link (the call to action).
```

**Adding a page:**
```
Add a /contact page with a hero section and a contact details section. Reuse
the Hero section type from the homepage.
```

